<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramiro II of León</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 898–951</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramiro II was king of León from 931 to 951. In 939 he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-simancas">Battle of Simancas</a>, one of the greatest Christian victories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>, and pushed the frontier south to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tormes">Tormes</a>. He also contended with the ambitions of Fernán González, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, who gradually made his county independent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramiro-ii-of-leon.jpg" alt="Ramiro II of León" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramiro2Leon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ramiro was the son of Ordoño II and Elvira Menéndez. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias">Alfonso III of Asturias</a> was forced by his sons to abdicate, shortly before his death in 910, the kingdom fell into successional crises, was partitioned among his sons, and was reunited and divided again.</p><p>When Fruela II died in 925, Alfonso Fróilaz was crowned but could not extend his power and was marginalised by his cousins, the three sons of Ordoño II, who had the backing of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>. They partitioned their share: Sancho Ordóñez ruled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, Alfonso IV in León, and Ramiro in the newly conquered lands to the south, where the chronicler Ibn Hayyan located his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Sancho died in 929 Alfonso IV absorbed his kingdom, but in 931 Ramiro forced Alfonso IV to abdicate and had him and the three sons of Fruela II blinded so that they could not rule. He became king of León, and with it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, the scant Anales castellanos primeros being a primary source for his reign. He stood out as a military commander, expanded his lands south into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> and Ledesma, and founded or repopulated frontier strongholds such as Osma and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clunia">Clunia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> called him the Devil for his ferocity in battle.</p><p>Ramiro masterminded a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> and León that defeated a joint Andalusian counter-offensive at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-simancas">Battle of Simancas</a> in 939, which let the Leonese border advance from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Duero</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tormes">Tormes</a>. In his last years he lost the support of his brother-in-law and son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garcia-sanchez-i-of-pamplona">García Sánchez I of Pamplona</a>, who then helped Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernan-gonzalez-of-castile">Fernán González of Castile</a> gain brief de facto independence. In 950 Ramiro still led an expedition to the valley of the Edge and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> at Talavera.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ramiro died on 1 January 951. His first wife was Adosinda Gutiérrez, by whom he had Ordoño III, his successor, and his second was Urraca Sánchez of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, whose marriage brought an alliance and a son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-i-of-leon">Sancho I of León</a>.</p><p>These marriages set the stage for further succession conflict, with Ordoño and his son Vermudo II backed by the Galician nobility and Sancho and his son Ramiro III by Urraca's relatives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. Legend gave him a place in the poem Miragaia, which tells of a son by an Arab lord's daughter, ancestor of the Maia family, but modern scholars dismiss the Lara connections later attached to this tale.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramiro II of León” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abd al-Rahman III led summer campaigns in person, but in 939 he suffered a heavy defeat by <b>Ramiro II of León</b> at the Battle of Simancas, after which he rarely took the field himself.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ramiro II</b> (r. 931–951), allied with the count of Castile, Fernán González, defeated the caliph at the Battle of Simancas in 939, one of the few great Christian victories of the period, and resettled the Tormes valley around Salamanca.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Caliph of Córdoba's troops assaulted the walls of Zamora, held by men loyal to <b>Ramiro II of León</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-5">5 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The troops of King <b>Ramiro II of León</b> met those of the Cordovan caliph Abd al-Rahman III near the walls of Simancas, and Ramiro defeated the caliph's army.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-19">19 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 943 <b>Ramiro II of León</b> created the County of Monzón, around Monzón de Campos near Palencia, for Assur Fernández, a rival of Fernán González of Castile.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-monzon">County of Monzón</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Kingdom of Leon. Died in León, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.6,-5.57&amp;year=932" aria-label="León on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4415 649 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ramiro II of León"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4865 919h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4865 919h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=42.6,-5.57&amp;year=932">What was here in 932 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
